  "problem": "Suppose we wish to find $L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(\u03c4)\\,d\u03c4}$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{\u2032}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,d\u03c4=A(\u03c4)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(\u03c4)\\,d\u03c4}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \u21d2 & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & \u21d2 & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{\u2032}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{\u2032}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{\u2032}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{\u2032}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
